{{- /*
sudo-skip-guard.sh.tmpl — SUDO array + non-interactive declared skip.

Sets SUDO=() when already root (e.g. inside a chroot/container), else
SUDO=(sudo). When the script would have to prompt for a sudo password but
cannot (non-interactive stdin AND no cached credentials), it takes the DECLARED
early exit — skip.sh.tmpl, form skip_here, direction transient-blocking, probe
sudo-usable, site no-cached-sudo — so a CI/agent `chezmoi apply` never hangs on
an uncached sudo prompt.

WHY transient-blocking AND NOT harmless. This host IS eligible: it is simply
missing a credential that appears the moment someone runs an interactive apply
or authenticates sudo. Exiting non-zero instead is not an option — `chezmoi
apply` aborts every later script at the first script error — so the exit stays
0 and chezmoi still records the run as successful. What makes the skip
RECOVERABLE is the probe: each consumer hashes the cached `sudo-usable` token
(capabilities.tmpl, resolved once per command by the read-source-state.pre
hook) through fingerprint.tmpl's `values` interface, so the token flipping to
`available` changes that consumer's rendered content and chezmoi re-runs it by
itself. A consumer that inlines this guard MUST carry that fingerprint value;
without it the skip is recorded as done and never re-runs.

The runtime test stays a runtime test. The cached token is a RENDER-time
fingerprint input only: it decides whether the script re-runs, never whether
this guard fires. sudo may be usable at render time and gone by the time the
script runs (and the reverse), so the `sudo -n true` check below is the only
thing allowed to answer "can this script elevate right now".

The declaration renders once per call site with that consumer as its `script`,
so each of the four consumers carries its own sudo-skip-guard/no-cached-sudo
instance and clears its own state entry.

Usage: {{ includeTemplate "sudo-skip-guard.sh.tmpl" "my-script-name" }}
*/ -}}
# Use sudo only when not already root (e.g. when invoked inside chroot/container).
if [[ "${EUID}" -eq 0 ]]; then
  SUDO=()
else
  SUDO=(sudo)

  # Skip cleanly when we would have to prompt for a password but can't.
  # `[[ -t 0 ]]` is true only when stdin is a TTY; chezmoi connects stdin to
  # its own when run interactively, so this is true under an interactive
  # `chezmoi apply` but false under agent/CI runs. `sudo -n true` succeeds
  # when sudo has cached credentials or when the user has password-less sudo
  # configured — in either case we can proceed without prompting. Only when
  # both fail do we give up.
  if [[ ! -t 0 ]] && ! sudo -n true 2>/dev/null; then
{{ includeTemplate "skip.sh.tmpl" (dict "owner" "sudo-skip-guard/no-cached-sudo" "form" "skip_here" "script" . "site" "no-cached-sudo" "direction" "transient-blocking" "probe" "sudo-usable" "reason" "skipped (non-interactive shell, no cached sudo credentials)") | trim | indent 4 }}
  fi
fi
